  "answer": "The idea that humans only use 10% of their brain is a persistent myth with no scientific basis. In reality, **virtually all parts of the brain have known functions, and we use nearly 100% of it over the course of a day**—though not all regions are active simultaneously.",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "fact": "Neuroimaging (fMRI, PET scans) shows activity throughout the brain even during simple tasks or rest.",
      "explanation": "Different neural networks activate depending on the activity (e.g., speaking, solving math, resting), but no large area is completely silent or unused."
    },
    {
      "fact": "Brain damage studies confirm this: injuries to even small regions (e.g., stroke, trauma) often cause specific, measurable deficits (loss of movement, sensation, language, etc.).",
      "explanation": "If 90% were unused, damage to those areas would have no effect—but this is never observed."
    },
    {
      "fact": "The brain is metabolically expensive: it uses ~20% of the body's energy despite being only 2% of its weight.",
      "explanation": "Evolution would not sustain such a costly organ if most of it were unnecessary."
    },
    {
      "fact": "The myth likely originated from misinterpretations of early neuroscience (e.g., William James' comments on 'untapped potential'), glial cell research (which outnumber neurons but support them), or self-help rhetoric in the 20th century.",
      "explanation": "It persists because it’s appealing—suggesting hidden reserves of intelligence or creativity—but it is unequivocally false."
    }
  ],
  "accurate_statement": "Humans use 100% of their brain, with different regions specialized for different functions and activated as needed."
}
